      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lactobacillus bacteria ferment sugars in the dough to produce lactic and acetic acid, which creates the sour flavor and provides wanderproofing against unwanted molds. Meanwhile, wild yeast and other microorganisms consume those remaining sugars and consume carbon dioxide gas, which gets trapped in the gluten network to make the dough rise and create airy holes. This symbiotic relationship ensures a stable, rising dough with a distinctive tangy taste and improved shelf life compared to quick-rise breads.",
